#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U3")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U3.1 Annihilators",[Compute the annihilator of $[4]$ in the $ZZ$-module $ZZ slash 12ZZ$.],[Solve $4r=0$ modulo twelve.],[This holds exactly when three divides $r$.],[The annihilator is $3ZZ$, because $12|4r$ iff $3|r$. The cyclic submodule generated by $[4]$ is consequently isomorphic to $ZZ slash 3ZZ$.],[Specify the element annihilator rather than the whole-module annihilator.]),
 p(2,"E","U3.1 Module maps",[Describe all $ZZ$-module maps $ZZ -> ZZ slash 5ZZ$.],[The image of one determines the map.],[Any of the five residue classes can be chosen.],[For each $a in ZZ slash 5ZZ$, the map is $n|->n a$. These are additive and hence $ZZ$-linear, and every map has this form by additivity. There are five.],[Explain existence and exhaustiveness.]),
 p(3,"E","U3.2 Submodules",[Show an ideal of $R$ is exactly a submodule of $R$ as an $R$-module.],[Compare the definitions.],[Both require an additive subgroup closed under multiplication by every scalar.],[A submodule of the regular module is an additive subgroup with $r a$ in it whenever $r in R$ and $a$ belongs. In a commutative ring this is exactly an ideal's absorption condition. Thus the notions coincide.],[State the role of commutativity if discussing two-sided ideals.]),
 p(4,"E","U3.2 Quotients",[Describe the quotient $ZZ^2/N$ for $N=\{(a,0):a in ZZ\}$.],[Project to the second coordinate.],[The projection is onto with kernel $N$.],[The map $(a,b)|->b$ is a surjective module homomorphism with kernel $N$. Thus the quotient is isomorphic to $ZZ$, by the first module isomorphism theorem.],[Provide the map rather than an unsupported dimension argument.]),
 p(5,"C","U3.1 Cyclic homomorphisms",[Compute the number of $ZZ$-module maps $ZZ slash m ZZ -> ZZ slash n ZZ$.],[The image of one must be annihilated by $m$.],[Count residues $a$ with $n|m a$.],[A map is uniquely determined by $a=f([1])$ and is well defined exactly when $m a=0$ modulo $n$. Writing $d=gcd(m,n)$, this means $a$ is a multiple of $n/d$ modulo $n$. There are exactly $d$ such residues, hence $d$ homomorphisms.],[Prove the condition is sufficient as well as necessary.]),
 p(6,"C","U3.1 Operator modules",[Let $T$ on $K^2$ satisfy $T(e_1)=0,T(e_2)=e_1$. Show its $K[x]$-module is cyclic and identify it as a quotient.],[The vector $e_2$ generates $e_1$ under $x$.],[The annihilator of $e_2$ is $(x^2)$.],[The vectors $e_2$ and $x e_2=e_1$ span, so the module is cyclic. For $f=a_0+a_1x+x^2q$, $f(T)e_2=a_0e_2+a_1e_1$, which vanishes exactly when $a_0=a_1=0$. Thus the annihilator is $(x^2)$ and the module is $K[x] slash (x^2)$.],[Compute the full annihilator, not only one annihilating polynomial.]),
 p(7,"C","U3.2 Isomorphism theorem",[In $ZZ$, take submodules $A=4ZZ$ and $B=6ZZ$. Identify $(A+B) slash B$ and $A slash (A inter B)$.],[Compute gcd and lcm ideals.],[The sum is $2ZZ$ and intersection is $12ZZ$.],[The two modules are $2ZZ slash 6ZZ$ and $4ZZ slash 12ZZ$, each cyclic of order three. The isomorphism sends the class of an element of $4ZZ$ modulo $12ZZ$ to its class modulo $6ZZ$. Its kernel and image are exactly those in the second isomorphism theorem.],[State the induced map and distinguish the ambient quotients.]),
 p(8,"C","U3.2 Simple modules",[Classify the simple $ZZ$-modules up to isomorphism.],[Use maximal ideals of $ZZ$.],[They are generated by primes.],[Every simple module over a commutative ring is $R slash M$ for a maximal ideal. The maximal ideals of $ZZ$ are $p ZZ$ with $p$ prime. Thus the simple $ZZ$-modules are exactly $ZZ slash p ZZ$. Different primes give different cardinalities.],[Exclude the zero module by the definition of simple.]),
 p(9,"C","U3.1 Torsion",[For a domain $R$, prove the torsion elements of an $R$-module form a submodule.],[Choose a nonzero annihilator for each of two elements.],[The product of the annihilators is still nonzero.],[If $a m=0$ and $b n=0$ with nonzero $a,b$, then $a b(m+n)=0$. Since $R$ is a domain, $a b!=0$. Also $a(r m)=r(a m)=0$ and $a(-m)=0$. Zero is torsion, so these elements form a submodule.],[The domain hypothesis ensures the product annihilator is nonzero.]),
 p(10,"A","U3.1 Maps from a quotient",[Prove naturally that $"Hom"_R(R slash I,M)$ is isomorphic to $\{m in M:I m=0\}$ as an $R$-module.],[Evaluate a map at $[1]$.],[Reconstruct by $[r]|->r m$.],[Evaluation at $[1]$ sends a linear map to an element annihilated by $I$, since $i[1]=0$. Conversely, if $I m=0$, the formula $[r]|->r m$ is representative-independent and linear. The two constructions are inverse and preserve addition and scalars. Postcomposition with a module map commutes with evaluation, giving naturality in $M$.],[Check well-definedness and the naturality statement.]),
 p(11,"A","U3.2 Splitting a torsion module",[Prove $ZZ slash 12ZZ iso ZZ slash 3ZZ oplus ZZ slash 4ZZ$ as modules, and identify the images of a generator.],[Use simultaneous reduction.],[Coprimality gives surjectivity by Bézout.],[The map $[a]_{12}|->([a]_3,[a]_4)$ is well defined with trivial kernel, because an integer divisible by three and four is divisible by twelve. Both sides have twelve elements, so it is onto; alternatively $4r-3s$ lifts $([r]_3,[s]_4)$. The generator $[1]$ maps to $([1],[1])$.],[Check the lifting formula modulo each denominator.]),
 p(12,
  "A",
  "U3.1 Submodule invariance",
  [For a vector space with operator $T$, prove its $K[x]$-submodules are exactly its $T$-invariant vector subspaces.],
  [Constants in $K[x]$ supply scalar multiplication.],
  [Closure under $T$ gives closure under every polynomial in $T$.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The indeterminate $x$ acts as the operator $T$. All other polynomial actions are built from that one operator and the already available vector-space operations.

*Argument.* A $K[x]$-submodule is a $K$-subspace and is closed under multiplication by $x$, hence under $T$. Conversely a $K$-subspace stable under $T$ is stable under every power of $T$ by induction, and under every finite linear combination of these powers. Thus it is stable under all $f(T)$ and is a submodule.

If $W$ is invariant under $T$, induction gives $T^j(W) subset W$ for every nonnegative integer $j$, including $j=0$ because $T^0=id$. For $f(x)=sum_(j=0)^n a_j x^j$ and $w in W$, each $a_j T^j(w)$ lies in $W$ and their finite sum does too. This verifies closure under every scalar in $K[x]$. In the reverse direction, constant polynomials supply all $K$-scalar operations, while the polynomial $x$ supplies $T$.

*A tempting dead end.* Closure under a single application of $T$ is insufficient unless vector-space closure is also established. A set of eigenvectors need not itself be closed under addition.

*Hypotheses.* Use the specified module structure $f dot v=f(T)v$. For a different action of $K[x]$, the associated operator could be different.],
  [Prove both implications and explain the polynomial action.],
  idea: [Reduce stability under an entire polynomial ring to stability under its scalar field and its one algebra generator.]),
 p(13,
  "A",
  "U3 Transfer: A single nilpotent chain",
  [On $K^3$ let $T(e_1)=0$, $T(e_2)=e_1$, and $T(e_3)=e_2$. Determine all T-invariant vector subspaces, with proof that your list is exhaustive.],
  [Choose a vector in an invariant subspace with its highest nonzero coordinate as far to the right as possible.],
  [Apply powers of T, then subtract the lower coordinate terms.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The operator moves every coordinate down one position. A highest nonzero coordinate therefore forces an entire initial segment of the basis into the subspace.

*Argument.* The zero subspace is invariant. If a nonzero invariant subspace $W$ has a vector $a e_1+b e_2+c e_3$ with $c!=0$, applying $T^2$ gives $c e_1$, so $e_1 in W$. Applying $T$ and subtracting $b e_1$ gives $c e_2$, so $e_2 in W$. Subtracting the first two coordinates of the original vector gives $c e_3$, so $W=K^3$. If no such vector exists but one has $b!=0$, the same two-step argument gives $e_1,e_2 in W$, and $W$ is their span. If every vector has only its first coordinate nonzero, a nonzero one spans $K e_1$. Thus the complete list is zero, $K e_1$, $K e_1+K e_2$, and $K^3$, all visibly invariant.

*A tempting dead end.* Not every line is invariant. A line through $e_2$ already fails because its image contains $e_1$.

*Hypotheses.* Nonzero coefficients can be inverted because K is a field. The operator is one chain; a direct sum of multiple chains would allow additional invariant subspaces.],
  [Prove exhaustiveness as well as invariance, treating all three possible highest coordinates.],
  optional: true,
  minutes: 60,
  idea: [A highest nonzero coordinate generates all lower coordinates under repeated application and subtraction.]),
)
